    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, vinegar kills weeds, but only the green parts it touches. Acetic acid burns leaves and stems on contact and does not travel down to the roots, so young annual weeds die and established perennials like dandelion, bindweed and thistle grow straight back. Kitchen vinegar at 5% is too weak to be reliable on anything past the seedling stage. Horticultural vinegar at 20% works far better and is a genuine chemical hazard that needs goggles and gloves. Skip the salt, add a little soap, and use it where a burn-down is all you need.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: It Kills Leaves, Not Roots
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinegar is a contact herbicide. The acetic acid in it breaks down the waxy outer layer of a leaf and ruptures the cell membranes underneath, so the tissue leaks water and dries out. On a sunny afternoon you can watch it happen: treated leaves darken and go limp within an hour or two and turn brown and crisp by the next morning. That speed is why vinegar has such a loyal following. It looks like it worked, and on the part of the plant you sprayed, it did.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is in the word contact. Acetic acid does not move through the plant the way a systemic herbicide does. It kills the cells it touches and stops there. Anything below the soil line, the taproot of a dandelion, the creeping rhizomes of quackgrass, the deep runners of field bindweed, never sees the acid. A perennial weed treats a vinegar spray the way it treats a lawn mower: it loses its top growth, draws on the energy stored in its roots, and pushes up new leaves within a week or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best data on this comes from the USDA Agricultural Research Service, whose researchers in Beltsville, Maryland tested vinegar on common lambsquarters, giant foxtail, velvetleaf, smooth pigweed and Canada thistle. Lower concentrations killed annual weeds during the first two weeks after they emerged, and older plants needed stronger solutions. On Canada thistle, a deep-rooted perennial, the vinegar burned down every shoot and the roots were unaffected, so the shoots always regrew. That one result explains most of the disappointment people have with vinegar.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the useful version of the question is not whether vinegar kills weeds but which weeds it kills for good. The answer is small annual weeds, ideally seedlings, sprayed in sun with a strong enough product. Everything else, it only sets back. Our guide to the <a href="/best-weed-killer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best weed killers</a> saw exactly this in testing: a 20% vinegar product browned a bindweed patch in a day and had fresh shoots back within two weeks, while a systemic product on the next strip kept it clear for the season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strength" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 5% vs 20%: Strength Changes Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not all vinegar is the same product. The number that matters is the percentage of acetic acid, and it ranges from about 5% in the white vinegar in your kitchen to 20% or more in products sold for weed control. Cleaning vinegar sits in between at around 6%. Apple cider vinegar is about the same strength as white vinegar, so it is no stronger as a weed killer despite the folklore around it. The color and the source fruit make no difference; only the acid content does.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Acetic acid</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it reliably kills</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hazard level</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kitchen white or cider vinegar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tiny seedlings with one or two true leaves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild eye irritant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cleaning vinegar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seedlings, slightly more reliably</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild eye irritant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Horticultural vinegar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10% to 20%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Young annual weeds, burn-down of perennial tops</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can burn skin and injure eyes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-strength vinegar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to about 30%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same weeds, faster and on older leaves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Corrosive, full PPE</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The ARS work put numbers on the gap. Household-strength 5% vinegar did not give reliable control, while solutions of 10, 15 and 20% gave roughly 80 to 100 percent control of annual weeds like foxtail, lambsquarters, pigweed and velvetleaf. At the higher concentrations the kill rate stayed in the 85 to 100 percent range across growth stages. In practical terms, kitchen vinegar is a seedling spray. If the weed has more than a couple of true leaves, you need horticultural strength or a different method.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a legal wrinkle worth knowing before you buy. Acetic acid products sold to kill weeds are pesticides, and in the US they are supposed to carry an EPA registration number on the label. Registered 20% acetic acid herbicides do exist, and they carry the signal word DANGER because of the eye and skin hazard. Some horticultural vinegars are sold with a garden image on the bottle but no registration, which is why at least one state agency has issued warnings about vinegar marketed as an herbicide. Look for the registration number and follow that label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stronger is not automatically better for you, either. Going from 20% to 30% buys a faster burn on tougher, older leaves, but it does nothing for roots, and the handling risk climbs steeply. For most homeowners treating a patio, a path or a few driveway cracks, a registered 20% product is the sensible ceiling. If a 20% spray is not killing your weeds, the problem is almost always that they are perennials, and more acid will not change that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Horticultural Vinegar Is Not Kitchen Vinegar
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part most natural weed killer articles skip. Purdue Extension notes that vinegar solutions of 11 percent or greater strength can cause skin burns and eye injury, and University of Illinois Extension warns that high concentrations can cause irreversible damage to skin and eyes. The fact that acetic acid is the same chemical you put on chips does not make a 20% solution gentle. It is closer to a pool chemical than a condiment, and the label signal word on registered products reflects that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear real protection. That means chemical splash goggles, not sunglasses or ordinary safety glasses with open sides, because a sprayer that kicks back or a gust of wind will put droplets in your eyes. Add nitrile or other chemical-resistant gloves, long sleeves and closed shoes. Spray on a still day with the wind at your back, keep the nozzle low and on a coarse setting, and never spray above waist height where drift reaches your face. Keep children and pets away until the treated area has dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fumes are sharp at 20% and stronger, especially in a pump sprayer you open after it has been sitting in the sun. Open it outdoors and point it away from you. Do not mix vinegar with chlorine bleach under any circumstances: acid plus bleach releases chlorine gas, and a homemade weed spray is a common way people end up doing exactly that. Do not add other household chemicals to make it stronger. Vinegar plus a little soap is the only mixture worth making.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your equipment needs attention too. Concentrated acetic acid attacks some metals and rubber seals over time, so use a plastic pump sprayer reserved for vinegar, rinse it with clean water after each session, and store it unpressurized. Keep the jug in its original labeled container, sealed, away from food and out of reach. If you get it on your skin, rinse with plenty of water for several minutes. If you get it in your eyes, flush for at least fifteen minutes and get medical help.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Treat anything above 10% acetic acid like a chemical, not a condiment.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Goggles, gloves, sleeves, a still day and a dedicated plastic sprayer. Never mix it with bleach. If a product sold as weed killer has no EPA registration number on the label, choose one that does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-kills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Vinegar Kills and What Shrugs It Off
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinegar does its best work on young, soft annual weeds. Crabgrass seedlings in a patio joint, chickweed in a path, lambsquarters and pigweed coming up in a bare bed, and the general green fuzz that appears in gravel after a wet spring all die readily, especially if you catch them in their first two or three weeks. Annuals live and die by their top growth. Burn it off before they have stored much energy and they have nothing left to regrow from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Established perennials are a different story. Dandelions regrow from the taproot. Field bindweed and Canada thistle regrow from deep, spreading roots, and even small root fragments sprout. Quackgrass and other creeping grasses regrow from rhizomes, and nutsedge from underground tubers. Grasses in general are hard to kill with any contact spray because their growing point sits at or below the soil surface, out of reach. Repeated spraying every time new leaves appear can eventually exhaust a perennial, but it takes many rounds across a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Conditions matter almost as much as the weed. Vinegar works fastest in warm, sunny, dry weather, when leaves are actively transpiring and the damaged tissue dries out quickly. Spray in the morning of a clear day and you will usually see results by afternoon. Spray on a cool, cloudy day or just before rain and the effect is slower and weaker, and rain will dilute and wash off what is on the leaf. Thorough coverage matters too, because any leaf you miss keeps feeding the plant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weeds with waxy or hairy leaves are harder for vinegar to penetrate, and succulent weeds like purslane hold enough water to shrug off a light spray. This is where adding a small amount of soap as a surfactant helps, and where a second application a few days later earns its keep. If you are working on hardscape rather than beds, our guide on <a href="/how-to-kill-weeds-in-driveway-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to kill weeds in driveway cracks</a> covers where vinegar fits next to pulling, flame and residual products, and why sealing the crack is the real fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, vinegar is completely non-selective. It will burn lawn grass, vegetable seedlings and ornamental leaves just as readily as weeds. Use it on hardscape, paths and bare ground, or with a shield around anything you want to keep. If your weeds are in the lawn itself, vinegar will leave a brown patch where every grass blade it touched used to be, and a selective lawn herbicide plus a thicker, well-fed lawn is the better answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="salt-and-soap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Salt and Dish Soap: One Helps, One Hurts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most popular homemade recipe on the internet is vinegar, salt and dish soap. Two of those three ingredients are reasonable. The soap is a surfactant: it lowers surface tension so the spray spreads across the leaf instead of beading up and rolling off, which helps acetic acid get through the waxy cuticle. It is not the best surfactant available, but a small squirt per gallon, commonly about a tablespoon, measurably improves coverage on waxy weeds. More soap does not help and leaves a foamy mess.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt is the ingredient to leave out. Sodium chloride does kill plants, which is why it has been used for that purpose since antiquity, but it does not break down. It dissolves, moves with water through the soil, and accumulates, damaging soil structure and blocking water movement. The vinegar in the mix is gone from the soil within days. The salt stays until heavy watering leaches it away, which can take a long time in clay, and on its way out it can reach your lawn edge or a nearby bed.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is what people mean when they say salt sterilises soil. It is not permanent in a strict sense, and extension specialists note that salt damage can be remediated with repeated deep soakings, but it is far more durable than anything vinegar does. On a driveway or patio it has a second cost: salt contributes to surface scaling on concrete, the same damage road salt does in winter. A weed killer that roughens your slab and kills the grass along its edge is not a good trade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epsom salt, which shows up in some recipes, is magnesium sulfate rather than sodium chloride. It is a plant nutrient, not a weed killer, so adding it mostly feeds whatever you are trying to kill. The sensible recipe is short: a registered horticultural vinegar used at label strength, with a little soap if the product does not already contain a surfactant. Many ready-to-use 20% products already include one, so check the label before you add anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-glyphosate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Vinegar vs Glyphosate and Other Systemics
          </h2>

          <p>
            The real comparison is not vinegar against nothing. It is vinegar against the other tools on the shelf, and each one works differently. Glyphosate, the active ingredient in Roundup concentrates, is systemic: the leaves absorb it and the plant moves it down to the roots over roughly a week, which is why it kills perennials that vinegar only sets back. Diquat, the active in Spectracide Weed and Grass Killer, is a synthetic contact killer like vinegar, fast but top-growth only. Selective lawn herbicides kill broadleaf weeds without killing turf.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example active</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reaches roots</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Visible results</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Horticultural vinegar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acetic acid, 20%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Young annuals on hardscape, organic gardens</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Synthetic contact</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diquat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hours to a day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quick burn-down of annuals</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Non-selective systemic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glyphosate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 to 14 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Perennials, bindweed, quackgrass</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Residual vegetation killer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glyphosate plus imazapyr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, plus soil barrier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 to 14 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gravel, cracks, fence lines</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Selective lawn herbicide</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mesotrione</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 to 2 weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weeds in cool-season turf</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Speed is the reason people pick vinegar, and it is also its trap. Vinegar shows a dead-looking weed in hours. Glyphosate shows nothing for days, and impatient users re-spray it at day three thinking it failed. But a week later the glyphosate-treated perennial is dying from the root up while the vinegar-treated one is putting out new leaves. If you judge by the first 48 hours, vinegar wins every time. If you judge by the same spot a month later, the systemic usually wins on anything with a real root system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinegar does have legitimate advantages. It leaves no meaningful soil residual, so you can replant soon after, and its effect on soil pH and microbes is minimal and short-lived. Many 20% products are listed for organic use, which matters in a vegetable garden or near food crops. It is the comfortable choice where you would rather not use a synthetic herbicide at all, such as around a pond, in a play area, or along a path the dog uses every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical approach many gardeners land on is to use both, in their place. Vinegar handles the quick, cosmetic jobs: seedlings in the patio, the strip along the garden path, the weeds that pop up after a rain. A systemic, applied carefully and according to its label, handles the perennial problem spots once or twice a year. For the lawn itself, neither one belongs; a selective herbicide and feeding with one of the <a href="/best-fertilizer-for-grass/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best lawn fertilizers</a> so the grass crowds weeds out is the long-term fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Cost Per Square Foot Reality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinegar looks cheap because kitchen vinegar is cheap. The useful number is cost per square foot per season, and that depends on strength, coverage and how many times you have to come back. At the time of writing, a gallon of a 20% ready-to-use product like Green Gobbler ran roughly 19 to 29 dollars depending on the retailer, and the maker claims coverage of up to about 1,000 square feet per gallon. That works out to around two to three cents per square foot for a single pass at the label rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice you use more than that. Wetting dense, leafy growth thoroughly enough to kill it takes more volume than a light mist, and a second pass a few days later on anything that survived is common. Then there is the regrowth. A perennial patch needs another spray every time new leaves appear, which in a growing season can mean four to six rounds. Multiply a few cents per square foot by six and a modest 200 square foot gravel area becomes a surprisingly expensive habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with a glyphosate concentrate. Roundup Super Concentrate is mixed at about 2.5 fluid ounces per gallon of water for tough weeds, and the label puts one mixed gallon at roughly 300 square feet. Per pass, the cost lands in the same few-cents range as vinegar. The difference is the number of passes: one or two applications that reach the roots against a season of repeat burn-downs. On perennial weeds, the systemic is usually cheaper as well as more effective.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kitchen vinegar is the false economy. It costs very little per gallon, but on anything past the seedling stage it does not reliably kill, so you spray repeatedly for a partial result. Where vinegar earns its cost is small areas of young annual weeds, organic beds, and places where you have decided not to use synthetics. If you only have a few square feet of cracks, a pump bottle of 20% is fine. If you are fighting a whole driveway, read on to the crack sealing step, because the cheapest weed is the one that has nowhere to grow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two other costs belong in the math. One is your time, since repeat spraying every couple of weeks adds up over a summer. The other is hardscape damage: concentrated acid on concrete reacts with the cement and can dull or etch the surface with repeated use, and it will etch limestone, marble and travertine. Rinse splashes off stone, and for paver joints, filling with a good <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">polymeric sand</a> does more long-term than any spray.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Kitchen Vinegar on Established Weeds</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Five percent vinegar is a seedling spray. On a dandelion with a full rosette or a clump of grass in a crack, it browns a few leaves and the plant carries on. If the weed is past its first couple of weeks, use a registered 20% product or switch to pulling or a systemic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Salt to Make It Stronger</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Salt does not break down. It builds up in the soil, moves with water into the lawn edge and nearby beds, and contributes to surface scaling on concrete. Vinegar is gone from the soil within days. Leave the salt in the kitchen and add only a little soap for coverage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Goggles With 20% Vinegar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horticultural vinegar at 11% and above can burn skin and injure eyes, and a pump sprayer or a gust of wind puts droplets exactly where you do not want them. Wear sealed splash goggles and chemical-resistant gloves every time, even for a quick spot treatment along the path.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Before Rain or on a Cloudy Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vinegar needs sun and dry leaves to do its work quickly. Rain within a few hours dilutes and rinses it off, and cool, overcast weather slows the burn. Pick a warm, clear, still morning with no rain forecast, and spray when the leaves are dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Spare the Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vinegar is non-selective. Overspray onto lawn grass, vegetable seedlings or ornamental leaves burns them as readily as the weed. Keep the nozzle low and on a coarse setting, shield anything you want to keep with a piece of cardboard, and never use it as a lawn weed killer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take vinegar to kill weeds?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a warm, sunny day, treated leaves usually wilt within an hour or two and look brown and dry within 24 hours. That is the top growth only. Annual seedlings stay dead, but established perennials typically push new leaves within one to two weeks, so check back then and retreat or switch to a method that reaches the roots.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinegar permanently kill weeds?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only annual weeds that are killed before they set seed. Vinegar does not move into the roots, so perennials such as dandelion, bindweed, thistle and quackgrass regrow from what is underground. Repeated spraying every time new growth appears can slowly exhaust them, but a systemic herbicide or digging out the roots is far more reliable for a permanent result.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will vinegar kill grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Vinegar kills any green tissue it touches, including lawn grass, though grass often regrows because its growing point sits at or below the soil. That makes vinegar poor for weeds in a lawn and poor for killing grass permanently in cracks. Use a selective lawn herbicide for weeds in turf, and keep vinegar to hardscape, paths and bare ground.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is vinegar weed killer safe for pets and kids?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Once it has dried, the treated area is generally considered low risk, and vinegar leaves no lasting residue. Wet, concentrated horticultural vinegar is a different matter: at 20% it can burn skin and eyes, including a dog nose or paws. Keep children and pets away during spraying and until the area is dry, and store the jug sealed and out of reach.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best homemade vinegar weed killer recipe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a registered 20% horticultural vinegar at label strength and add about a tablespoon of dish soap per gallon if the product does not already contain a surfactant. Skip salt, which builds up in soil and harms concrete, and never add bleach, which releases chlorine gas. Spray young weeds on a warm, sunny, still morning for the best result.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinegar change the soil pH?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for long. Acetic acid breaks down quickly in soil, and Purdue Extension notes it is not likely to accumulate enough to affect soil pH for more than a few days. That is one of the genuine advantages of vinegar over salt and over residual herbicides: you can usually replant a treated spot soon after the weeds have died back and been cleared.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is apple cider vinegar better than white vinegar for weeds?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Apple cider vinegar and white vinegar both contain about 5% acetic acid, and the acid content is the only thing that matters for killing weeds. Neither is reliable on anything past the seedling stage. If you want vinegar that genuinely works, buy horticultural vinegar at around 20%, and handle it with goggles and gloves.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

